KNOWNBUG
main.cpp
--cpp11
^\[main\.assertion\.1\] .* size: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.2\] .* no element destroyed yet: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.3\] .* element value: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.4\] .* destroyed with the variable: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.5\] .* WRONG \(must FAIL\): FAILURE$
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
^EXIT=10$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^warning: ignoring
^CONVERSION ERROR$
--
The backing array (initializer_list_backing_array, cpp_typecheck_
conversions.cpp) is a static-lifetime symbol; its class-type elements are
copied into it by its static initializer and never destroyed ("destroyed
with the variable" fails).  Giving the array the lifetime of the list
object -- a block-scope temporary whose elements are copy-constructed and
destroyed -- needs the lifetime machinery of [class.temporary]/6 for the
array, as for a temporary bound to a reference.  g++ 13
(-fsanitize=address,undefined) and clang++ 18 accept this program, and it
fails only its WRONG assertion.
